Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT DILG-NAPOLCOM Center, EDSA corner Quezon Avenue, West Triangle, Quezon City http: //www.dilg.com.ph 30 ZOZO OPINION NO. _ ATTY. VOLTAIRE C. DELA CRUZ City Administrator, City of Malabon Malabon City Hall F. Sevilla Blvd. Malabon, Metro Manila Dear Attty. Dela Cruz: S. 200 This has reference to your telephone inquiry with the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Community Participation and Barangay Affairs, this Department, requesting for a legal opinion on whether or not the demolition of illegal structures along Rimas Road, City of Malabon, by the Malabon City Government in compliance with DILG Memorandum Circular 2019-121, is contrary to Republic Act No. 7279, otherwise known as the "Urban Development and Housing Act of 1992". DILG Memorandum Circular 2019-121 is to be construed and complied with in harmony with R.A. No. 7279 and any other existing law for that matter. Thus, the answer to whether or not the compliance with DILG MC 2019-121 is contrary to, or in violation of, R.A. No. 7279, depends on the manner by which said compliance is made. We put emphasis on DILG Memorandum Circular No. 2019-121 being a general directive to conduct clearing operations of all structures occupying and/or obstructing public roads that are used for private end, including those previously allowed/permitted by the local government. In no way should this general directive be complied with in violation of any existing law, rules and regulations that prescribe the procedures of how clearing operations/demolition should be made. Simply put, the clearing operations directed by the subject circular should be implemented in conjunction with legalities and procedures on demolition. Attention should be directed to 4th Paragraph of the subject Memorandum Circular which states "(w)ithin 60 calendar days, significant results must be achieved, documented, and reported together with the efforts of the LGUs to sustain the same". It did not require strict completion of the clearing operation within that 60-day period to give way to the demolition of structures which will undergo longer procedures

and/or judicial intervention. In the latter case, it suffices that the initial steps taken and/or its development is reported within the 60-day period. Insofar as dwellings/structures or road obstructions are concerned, the demolition of which are covered by R.A. No. 7279, we further explain as follows: 1 11 For dwellings/structures constructed after the effectivity of R.A. NO. 7279, demolition is to be effected through SUMMARY EVICTION. This is pursuant to Section 2(1.0) of the IRR of R.A. No. 7279, which states that new squatter families whose structures were built after the effectivity of RA No. 7279 shall be subject for summary eviction. This same IRR provision was crafted pursuant to Section 44 of R.A. No. 7279, to wit: "Sec. 44. Moratorium on Eviction and Demolition. — There shall be a moratorium on the eviction of all program beneficiaries and on the demolition of their houses or dwelling units for a period of three (3) years from the effectivity of this Act: Provided, That the moratorium shall not apply to those persons who have constructed their structures after the effectivity of this Act and for cases enumerated in Section 28 hereof. " (Emphasis ours) For dwellings/structures constructed prior to the effectivity of R.A. No. 7279, demolition is governed by Section 28 thereof, which reads: "Sec. 28. Eviction and Demolition. — Eviction or demolition as a practice shall be discouraged. Eviction or demolition, however, may be allowed under the following situations: (b) (c) When persons or entities occupy danger areas such as esteros, railroad tracks, garbage dumps, riverbanks, shorelines, watenvays, and other public places such as sidewalks, roads, parks, and playgrounds; When government infrastructure projects with available funding are about to be implemented; or When there is a court order for eviction and demolition. In the execution of eviction or demolition orders involving underprivileged and homeless citizens, the following shall be mandatory: (1) (2) Notice upon the effected persons or entities at least thirty (30) days prior to the date of eviction or demolition; Adequate consultations on the matter of settlement with the duly designated representatives of the families to be resettled and the affected communities in the areas where they are to be relocated;

(3) (4) (5) (6) (7) (8) Presence of local government officials or their representatives during eviction or demolition; Proper identification of all persons taking part in the demolition; Execution of eviction or demolition only during regular office hours from Mondays to Fridays and during good weather, unless the affected families consent otherwise; No use of heavy equipment for demolition except for structures that are permanent and of concrete materials; Proper uniforms for members of the Philippine National Police who shall occupy the first line of law enforcement and observe proper disturbance control procedures; and Adequate relocation, whether temporary or permanent: Provided, however, That in cases of eviction and demolition pursuant to a court order involving underprivileged and homeless citizens, relocation shall be undertaken by the local government unit concerned and the National Housing Authority with the assistance of other government agencies within forty-five (45) days from service of notice of final judgment by the court, after which period the said order shall be executed: Provided, further, That should relocation not be possible within the said period, financial assistance in the amount equivalent to the prevailing minimum daily wage multiplied by sixty (60) days shall be extended to the affected families by the local government unit concerned. " We hope to have enlightened you on the matter. Thank you. Very truly yours, MARIV L C. SACENDONCILLO, CESO 111 Und rsecretary for Local Government DIR. MARIA LOURDES L. AGUSTIN, CESO 111 DILG Regional Office, National Capital Region cc: Lega 1/84
